{
  "version": 1,
  "language": "german",
  "chapter": 14,
  "title": "When the Calendar Stopped Naming",
  "drivablePrefix": 7,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:1aade3cb90b99bdd",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "GE-C09-juli",
      "sequence": 264,
      "title": "der Juli — \"July\"",
      "headword": "der Juli",
      "romanization": "der Juli",
      "gloss": "July — the first month in the year named after a man, and he named it himself",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:2b1167ca088e29db",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Six months named for gods, festivals and guesses. From here the calendar changes what it is doing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: der Juli",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "der Juli — \"July.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin Julius, and not a god: Julius Caesar. He rebuilt the Roman calendar into something that tracked the sun, and the month of his birth was renamed for him while he was alive to see it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Juni and Juli differ by one letter and one sound. Take them slowly as a pair: YOO-nee, YOO-lee."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "j-as-y — English y again, as in Januar and Juni."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ie-long-ee — and a long ee at the end."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Because the two are so close, German speakers arranging a date will often say Julei for July, stretching it away from Juni. You will hear it; you do not have to say it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"der Juli\" — YOO-lee",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"der Juli\" — YOO-lee]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the pair, slowly — \"Juni, Juli\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the pair, slowly — \"Juni, Juli\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "who it is — \"Julius Caesar, not a god\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: who it is — \"Julius Caesar, not a god\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"Juni, Juli.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"July.\" (Der Juli.) And \"June\"? (Der Juni.) Which one is a god? (Juni — Juno.) Which one is a man? (Juli — Caesar.) Next: the man who took the hint."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "GE-C09-august",
      "sequence": 265,
      "title": "der August — \"August\"",
      "headword": "der August",
      "romanization": "der August",
      "gloss": "August — the second and last month named for a man, and the last name the calendar ever gave",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:c848cf10868bea52",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Caesar took a month. His heir noticed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: der August",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "der August — \"August.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The emperor Augustus, Caesar's adopted son, who had the next month renamed for himself. Two men in a row, side by side in the year, and then it stops: no month after this one is named for anybody at all."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The weight falls on the second syllable in German — au-GUST — where English puts it on the first."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "au-diphthong — au is one gliding vowel, the ow of English how."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "st-initial-scht — the st here is inside the word, not at the start, so it stays a plain st. German's sht happens at the beginning of a word, and this is the contrast that shows you where the boundary is."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"der August\" — au-GUST",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"der August\" — au-GUST]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the two men, in order — \"Juli, August\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the two men, in order — \"Juli, August\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "what happens next — \"the naming stops\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: what happens next — \"the naming stops\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"Juli, August.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"August.\" (Der August.) Which syllable is stressed? (The second — au-GUST.) Who is it? (The emperor Augustus.) How many months are named for men? (Two — and they are next to each other.) Next: what the calendar does once it runs out of names."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "GE-C09-september",
      "sequence": 266,
      "title": "der September — \"September\"",
      "headword": "der September",
      "romanization": "der September",
      "gloss": "September — the seventh month, which is now the ninth, and the name never caught up",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:670b35bdd6d14741",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have the Latin number for seven from a chapter about counting. Here it is again, wearing a month."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: der September",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "der September — \"September.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin september, from septem — seven. Count from März, where the Roman year started, and September lands seventh."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The year was later moved to start in January. The name was not moved with it, so a word meaning \"seventh\" now sits in the ninth slot, in German exactly as in English, and has done for two thousand years."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vowel-e-german — three clear short es: sep-TEM-ber."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "r-final — closing on the soft r."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The stress is on the middle syllable, as in English."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"der September\" — sep-TEM-ber",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"der September\" — sep-TEM-ber]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the Latin number inside it — \"septem, seven\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the Latin number inside it — \"septem, seven\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the German seven beside it — \"sieben\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the German seven beside it — \"sieben\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "where the count starts — \"März\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: where the count starts — \"März\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"September — septem — sieben.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"September.\" (Der September.) What number is inside it? (Septem — seven.) Which position does it actually hold now? (Ninth.) Why the mismatch? (The year used to start in März.) What is German's own seven? (Sieben.) Next: eight."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "GE-C09-oktober",
      "sequence": 267,
      "title": "der Oktober — \"October\"",
      "headword": "der Oktober",
      "romanization": "der Oktober",
      "gloss": "October — octo, eight, and German spells the Latin c as a k because German spells that sound with a k",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:5e9272835150c1e4",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The same trick as September, one number along."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: der Oktober",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "der Oktober — \"October.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin october, from octo — eight. Eighth from März, tenth on the calendar you use."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Notice the spelling. English writes Oc-, keeping the Latin c. German writes Ok-, because German spells that sound with a k and applies its own rule to a borrowed word. Same word, same origin, one letter of local habit."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vowel-o-german — a clean rounded o, held steady rather than gliding as English oh does."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "r-final — and the soft closing r."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ok-TOH-ber, weight in the middle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"der Oktober\" — ok-TOH-ber",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"der Oktober\" — ok-TOH-ber]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the Latin number — \"octo, eight\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the Latin number — \"octo, eight\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the German eight — \"acht\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the German eight — \"acht\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the pair so far — \"September, Oktober\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the pair so far — \"September, Oktober\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"Oktober — octo — acht.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"October.\" (Der Oktober.) Which letter does German use where English keeps a c? (A k.) What number is inside? (Octo — eight.) German's own eight? (Acht.) Next: nine."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "GE-C09-november",
      "sequence": 268,
      "title": "der November — \"November\"",
      "headword": "der November",
      "romanization": "der November",
      "gloss": "November — novem, nine, and a word German and English spell identically",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:8de7256fa3c59c28",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spelled exactly as in English. Said differently, on one letter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: der November",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "der November — \"November.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin november, from novem — nine. Ninth from März, eleventh now."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "German and English write this month with the same eleven letters. That is rarer than it looks, and it happens here because both took the word from Latin without touching it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "v-as-f — German v is an f. The month is no-FEM-ber."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "r-final — soft closing r, as on the two before it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is the one letter where the identical spelling stops being identical. A German reading November aloud produces something an English ear does not expect, and nothing about the page warns you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"der November\" — no-FEM-ber",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"der November\" — no-FEM-ber]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the Latin number — \"novem, nine\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the Latin number — \"novem, nine\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the German nine — \"neun\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the German nine — \"neun\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "three in order — \"September, Oktober, November\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: three in order — \"September, Oktober, November\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"November — no-FEM-ber.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"November.\" (Der November — no-FEM-ber.) How is German v said? (As an f.) What number is inside? (Novem — nine.) German's own nine? (Neun.) Next: ten, and the end of the year."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "GE-C09-dezember",
      "sequence": 269,
      "title": "der Dezember — \"December\"",
      "headword": "der Dezember",
      "romanization": "der Dezember",
      "gloss": "December — decem, ten, the last month and the last of the four numbered ones",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:225e80b04c43c6b9",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The last month, and a letter whose sound you were given with a number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: der Dezember",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "der Dezember — \"December.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin december, from decem — ten. Tenth from März, twelfth now, and the last of the four months that are nothing but numbers."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Where English writes De-c-, German writes De-z-."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "z-ts — German z is ts, the sound you were given on zehn. So the month opens de-TSEM-, not de-SEM-."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "r-final — and it closes on the same soft r as the other three."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is the whole difference between the German and English versions of one Latin word: c became z, because German already had a letter for that sound."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"der Dezember\" — de-TSEM-ber",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"der Dezember\" — de-TSEM-ber]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the German ten, same opening sound — \"zehn\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the German ten, same opening sound — \"zehn\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the Latin number — \"decem, ten\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the Latin number — \"decem, ten\"]"
            },
